    Harmony of the Great Lakes: A Water Level Balance
                       Approach
                                       Summary




                                                                       型
   The Great Lakes represent 20% of the world’s freshwater resources, making their
management, especially in water level, critically important. Fluctuations in their water
levels are influenced by a myriad of natural factors and involve a diverse group of




                                                        模
stakeholders. Consequently, developing a management plan and controlling the water
levels of these lakes present a challenge.
   For Task1: A genetic algorithm model is established considering coastal protection




                                                       学
costs, hydro-power generation, leisure satisfaction, shipping benefits, and ecological
impacts. By utilizing benefit functions and the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), this




                                               数
model efficiently assesses the interests of stakeholders. Besides, the model particular
focuses on coastal protection costs and hydroelectric power generation. It ultimately
establishes the optimal water level for the Great Lakes on a monthly basis.
                                ：
    For Task2: We develop a hydrodynamic model tailored to the Great Lakes, account-




                                                           els
ing for both the flow dynamics within the lake chain and external variables affecting it.
Following this, we craft distinct water level control algorithms for the Compensating
                               号
Works and the Moses-Saunders Dam. These algorithms integrate a mode-switchable



                                                         od
feedback controller with a prediction module, enhancing the controller’s responsive-
                        众

ness by optimizing feedback gains for superior performance amidst environmental
fluctuations. Simulations conducted using our model confirm the efficacy of these al-
                                                        m
gorithms.
                公


                                            H
    For Task 3: By increasing the outflow at the Soo Locks (upstream) and the Moses-
Saunders Dam (downstream), we evaluated their sensitivity to water level manage-
                                         AT

ment. Our analysis reveals that the upstream dam predominantly affects Lake Supe-
 信




rior, Lake Michigan, Lake Huron, and Lake Erie, whereas the downstream dam has
a more significant impact on the remaining lakes. When compared to water levels in
微




2017, our model demonstrates improved performance in numerous aspects.
                                  M



   For Task 4: We introduce dynamic scenarios into the simulation, including heavy
precipitation and ice jams, to assess the sensitivity and resilience of our model against
                         ：




environmental changes. The results show that our algorithms are more effective in
preventing significant increases in water levels following heavy rainfall events than
natural regulatory methods. Furthermore, when ice jam occurs, the time needed to
                 号




recover water levels decreases through enhanced upstream discharge.
   For Task 5: Focusing on the key factors and stakeholders affecting Lake Ontario, we
   信




identified the water flow from the Ottawa River and the protection of coastal residents
in Montreal as significant influences.The analysis emphasizes that to reach an effective
regulation requires seasonal adjustments and collaborative efforts to protect coastal
  微




communities and maintain ecological balance.
   In conclusion, our model has the strength of robustness and comprehensiveness.
This provides scientific guidance and support for the regulation of the Great Lakes in
the future.

   Keywords: Prediction-based feedback control, Lake chain simulation, Multi-objective
optimization, Genetic algorithm                                                     关注数学模型
